::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which choice best defines tone in speech communication?
{
=A. The attitude a speaker communicates through word choice and delivery#Correct. Tone is the attitude the audience hears through the speaker's language, voice, and delivery choices.
~B. The number of slides used in a presentation#This confuses tone with presentation materials. Slides may support a speech, but tone comes from the speaker's attitude and language.
~C. The exact place where the speech happens#This describes setting, not tone. Setting may affect choices, but tone is how the message sounds to the audience.
~D. The amount of time the speaker is allowed to talk#This describes time limit, not tone. A speaker can use an appropriate or inappropriate tone in any amount of time.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
A speaker is explaining a project to classmates. Which tone is most appropriate?
{
~A. Angry and insulting#This would make classmates defensive and distract from the message. A speaker should use a tone that helps the audience understand.
~B. Confusing and careless#This does not support communication. A class presentation needs clear language and a focused tone.
=C. Clear, respectful, and confident#Correct. This tone supports understanding, keeps the audience engaged, and shows that the speaker is prepared.
~D. Secretive and vague#This prevents the audience from understanding the message. Audience-aware speakers make their ideas clear.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which sentence best fits a formal email to a teacher?
{
~A. I need you to fix my grade today.#This sounds demanding and does not fit the audience. A formal message should make a respectful request.
=B. Could we please discuss my grade when you are available?#Correct. This sentence is respectful, clear, and appropriate for communicating with a teacher.
~C. My grade is bad and this is annoying.#This expresses frustration but does not make a clear or respectful request. Tone should support the purpose.
~D. Fix this because I do not like it.#This is too demanding. Audience-aware communication uses respectful language, especially with school staff.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which sentence uses the most precise academic vocabulary?
{
~A. The thing happened because of some stuff.#This is vague. Academic vocabulary should name the idea clearly so the audience can follow the explanation.
~B. The reason is just obvious to everyone.#This avoids precise explanation. Academic speaking should explain the reason with clear words.
~C. It did what it did because that is how it works.#This repeats unclear language. Replace vague words with specific terms tied to the topic.
=D. The outcome changed because the evidence showed a different pattern.#Correct. This sentence uses precise academic vocabulary such as outcome, evidence, and pattern.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
A student says to a group member, "That part of the presentation is terrible." Which revision best improves tone?
{
~A. That part is terrible, but I am saying it nicely.#This still uses insulting language. A respectful tone focuses on the work and offers a useful next step.
=B. This section may need clearer evidence. Can we revise it together?#Correct. This revision identifies the issue, keeps the tone respectful, and invites collaboration.
~C. I do not like it, so delete it.#This is too forceful and does not explain the communication problem. Good feedback gives a reason.
~D. Everyone will dislike your part.#This attacks the person and predicts audience reaction without support. Tone should help the group improve.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which presentation opening gives the audience the clearest focus?
{
=A. Today I will explain three ways speakers adjust tone for different audiences.#Correct. This opening states the topic and previews the organization, which helps the audience follow the presentation.
~B. I am going to talk for a while about several things.#This is too vague. The audience needs a clear topic and direction.
~C. I found some information, but I am not sure what it means yet.#This weakens the focus. A prepared speaker should guide the audience with a clear purpose.
~D. Before I begin, here is an unrelated story from last week.#This may distract the audience unless it connects directly to the presentation focus.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Why should a speaker consider purpose before choosing voice and tone?
{
~A. Purpose tells the speaker which words to avoid saying at all times.#This is too absolute. Purpose guides word choice, but it does not create one fixed list for every situation.
~B. Purpose matters only for written essays, not speech.#This is incorrect. Purpose matters whenever a person communicates, including speaking and presenting.
~C. Purpose lets the speaker ignore the audience's needs.#This reverses the lesson concept. Purpose and audience work together to shape effective communication.
=D. Purpose helps the speaker decide whether to inform, persuade, request, or discuss.#Correct. When the purpose is clear, the speaker can choose a tone and voice that fit the communication goal.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which message best matches a respectful tone for asking a classmate to speak more quietly?
{
~A. Stop being loud because you are ruining everything.#This sounds accusatory. A respectful request should name the need without attacking the person.
~B. You always talk too much and never listen.#This uses blaming language. Tone improves when the speaker focuses on the current need.
=C. Could you lower your voice a little so I can focus on the task?#Correct. This message is clear, respectful, and connected to the purpose of staying focused.
~D. I guess you can keep talking if you want to bother everyone.#This is sarcastic. Sarcasm often harms collaboration and weakens the message.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which word choice best supports academic communication in a presentation?
{
=A. evidence#Correct. Evidence is an academic word that helps a speaker explain how a claim is supported.
~B. stuff#This is too vague. Academic communication uses precise vocabulary so the audience understands the point.
~C. whatever#This sounds careless and unclear. It does not help explain an idea to an academic audience.
~D. thingy#This is informal and imprecise. A presenter should choose words that clearly name the idea.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which closing best supports a coherent oral presentation?
{
~A. That is all. I forgot the rest.#This does not reinforce the message. A strong closing should help the audience remember the main idea.
=B. In conclusion, audience, purpose, voice, and tone work together to make communication effective.#Correct. This closing restates the focus and connects the main lesson ideas clearly.
~C. I am done because the timer stopped.#This ends the speech but does not summarize the message. A closing should support understanding.
~D. My slides were about many different topics.#This is vague and does not show coherent focus. The audience needs a clear final takeaway.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
A speaker wants to persuade classmates to join a service project. Which tone would likely work best?
{
~A. Bored and uncertain#This tone would weaken the message. Persuasion usually needs energy and confidence.
~B. Harsh and demanding#This may push classmates away. Persuasive communication should still respect the audience.
=C. Encouraging and confident#Correct. This tone supports persuasion by showing belief in the idea while respecting classmates.
~D. Confused and hesitant#This makes the purpose unclear. A persuasive speaker should present the message with clear confidence.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which question should a speaker ask first when adjusting tone?
{
=A. Who is my audience, and what is my purpose?#Correct. Audience and purpose guide the speaker's choices about formality, voice, detail, and tone.
~B. How can I make the message sound the most dramatic?#This may distract from the purpose. Tone should fit the audience and goal, not just sound dramatic.
~C. How can I use the fewest words possible?#Being concise can help, but it is not the first question. The speaker must understand audience and purpose first.
~D. Which words will make me sound smarter than everyone else?#This focuses on impressing rather than communicating. Academic vocabulary should clarify ideas, not show off.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which sentence best uses academic vocabulary without sounding confusing?
{
~A. The topic is basically a bunch of things about talking.#This is too vague. Academic vocabulary should make the topic clearer.
~B. The communication thing has many pieces and stuff.#This uses imprecise words. Replace thing and stuff with exact terms.
~C. The speaker used fancy terminology to demonstrate superiority.#This may sound formal, but it does not clearly explain the lesson idea in student-friendly academic language.
=D. The speaker adjusted tone to match the audience and purpose.#Correct. This sentence uses precise vocabulary from the lesson while staying clear.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which statement shows the strongest understanding of voice?
{
~A. Voice means speaking louder than everyone else.#This confuses voice with volume. Voice includes the speaker's style, word choice, and way of expressing the message.
=B. Voice is the speaker's style or presence in the message.#Correct. Voice is how the speaker comes across through language, organization, and delivery choices.
~C. Voice means using slang in every situation.#This is too limited and often inappropriate. Voice should fit the audience and purpose.
~D. Voice means avoiding all personality when speaking.#This is incorrect. Voice can show personality while still being appropriate for the context.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which choice best supports logical organization in a speech?
{
~A. Place ideas randomly so the audience stays surprised.#This makes the speech harder to follow. Organization should help the audience understand.
~B. Repeat the same point without adding support.#This does not build the message. Logical organization develops ideas with support.
=C. Arrange ideas in an order that helps the audience follow the main point.#Correct. A coherent speech has a clear order that supports the speaker's focus.
~D. Skip transitions because the audience can guess the connections.#This creates confusion. Transitions help listeners understand how ideas connect.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
A student is speaking to younger students about hallway safety. Which tone is most appropriate?
{
~A. Sarcastic and annoyed#This would not help younger students learn. The tone should support understanding and cooperation.
~B. Overly technical and confusing#This does not fit younger students. Audience awareness requires language they can understand.
~C. Silent and unsure#This does not communicate the safety message clearly. The speaker needs a confident, helpful tone.
=D. Friendly, clear, and firm#Correct. This tone fits younger students because it is understandable, respectful, and serious enough for a safety topic.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which revision best changes an informal message into a more formal request?
{
=A. May I please have a few minutes to explain my concern?#Correct. This revision uses polite wording and a formal tone that fits a respectful request.
~B. I need to talk now, so listen.#This is demanding. A formal request should respect the listener's time and role.
~C. Hey, I have a problem or whatever.#This is too casual and unclear. Formal communication should be precise and respectful.
~D. You should already know what I want.#This is unclear and accusatory. The speaker should state the request directly.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Why is precise vocabulary important in speech communication?
{
~A. It lets the speaker avoid explaining ideas.#This is incorrect. Precise vocabulary should support explanation, not replace it.
~B. It allows the speaker to use words the audience cannot understand.#This works against communication. Good vocabulary choices clarify meaning for the audience.
~C. It makes every speech sound formal even when formal tone is wrong.#This overstates the idea. Vocabulary should match audience and purpose.
=D. It helps the audience understand exactly what the speaker means.#Correct. Precise words reduce confusion and make the message easier to follow.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which presentation plan best connects evidence to a main idea?
{
~A. Give evidence first and never explain what it proves.#This leaves the audience to guess. Evidence should be connected to the main idea.
~B. State a main idea but give no supporting details.#This is incomplete. A presentation needs support that develops the focus.
=C. State the main idea, present evidence, and explain how the evidence supports the idea.#Correct. This structure helps the audience understand both the claim and the support.
~D. Use evidence that is unrelated because it sounds interesting.#This weakens the presentation. Evidence should be relevant to the main idea.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which speaker choice best shows audience awareness?
{
~A. Using the same tone for a friend, teacher, and principal#This ignores audience differences. A speaker should adjust tone for different listeners.
~B. Choosing words only because they sound impressive#This may confuse the audience. Word choice should support understanding.
~C. Speaking without considering why the message matters#This ignores purpose. Audience awareness includes understanding why the speaker is communicating.
=D. Adjusting formality, detail, and tone to fit the listener and purpose#Correct. This choice shows that the speaker is thinking about both audience and purpose.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Read the comment: "Whatever. Your plan is fine, I guess." What is the main tone problem?
{
=A. It sounds dismissive and does not support collaboration.#Correct. Words like whatever and I guess can make the speaker sound uninterested or disrespectful.
~B. It is too formal for a group discussion.#This is not the main issue. The sentence is informal, but the bigger problem is its dismissive tone.
~C. It gives too much evidence.#This sentence gives no evidence. The issue is tone and lack of useful response.
~D. It explains the idea too carefully.#This sentence does not explain the idea. It gives a weak and unclear response.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which sentence best shows a respectful disagreement?
{
~A. That answer is bad, and you should stop saying it.#This attacks the person and does not explain the disagreement. Respectful disagreement focuses on ideas.
=B. I see it differently because the example points to another conclusion.#Correct. This sentence disagrees respectfully and gives a reason connected to evidence.
~C. You are wrong, and I am obviously right.#This is disrespectful and does not support the claim. Tone matters when disagreeing.
~D. I disagree, but I will not explain why.#This is incomplete. A respectful disagreement should include reasoning when possible.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.SPK.01
Question:
Which choice best describes a coherent focus?
{
~A. A speech that changes topics every few seconds#This lacks focus. Coherence means the ideas connect to one main message.
~B. A speech that includes every fact the speaker found#This can overwhelm the audience. Coherent focus requires selecting details that support the point.
=C. A speech where each part supports the main idea#Correct. Coherent focus means the introduction, support, and conclusion all connect to the same purpose.
~D. A speech with no introduction because the audience can guess the topic#This makes the focus harder to follow. An introduction helps orient the audience.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.05
Question:
Which revision replaces vague language with precise academic language?
{
~A. The speaker talked about some stuff.#This remains vague. The audience still does not know what the speaker explained.
~B. The speaker did a thing with words.#This is still unclear. Academic communication requires specific wording.
~C. The speaker was, like, really good at talking.#This is informal and imprecise. It does not name the skill being discussed.
=D. The speaker used evidence to support the main claim.#Correct. This revision uses precise academic vocabulary and clearly describes the communication skill.
}
::SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: SPEECH_U01_L02_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.SPEECH.COM.09
Question:
Which statement best summarizes Lesson 2?
{
=A. Effective speakers match audience, purpose, voice, and tone.#Correct. This is the central lesson idea: speakers make choices based on who is listening and why they are communicating.
~B. Effective speakers always use the most formal tone possible.#This is too rigid. Formal tone is useful in some situations, but tone must match the audience and purpose.
~C. Effective speakers ignore audience so the message stays personal.#This contradicts the lesson. Audience awareness is essential for effective communication.
~D. Effective speakers use academic words even when the audience cannot understand them.#This misuses academic vocabulary. Word choice should clarify the message for the audience.
}
